Task:
Produce a scoring rubric that determines whether the cohort label is
supported by evidence in the record.

The rubric must output one of:
- YES
- NO
- INSUFFICIENT EVIDENCE

CRITICAL DESIGN PRINCIPLES (must obey):

1) This is LABEL VALIDATION, not disease discovery.
   The question is:
   “Is the assigned phenotype label clinically plausible and internally
    consistent given the remaining claims evidence?”

2) Condition diagnosis codes are only moderate evidence.
   Specifically include Condition diagnosis codes for <PHENOTYPE_NAME> in the rubric as per the following criteria:
   <DX_CRITERA>
   NOTE: If using diagnosis codes for the rubric is permitted, only use this criteria in ONE rule.

3) Evidence types must be explicitly categorized.
   Prefer corroborating signals such as:
   - disease-specific procedures
   - disease-specific medications or supplies
   - peri-index management
   - post-index continuity of care

4) Single-signal validation is NOT allowed.
   A YES decision must require evidence from at least TWO DISTINCT
   evidence categories (e.g., procedure + medication).

5) Exclusion criteria MUST be explicitly modeled and evaluated BEFORE scoring.
   Exclusions are NOT negative evidence.

   There are two exclusion classes:
   A) HARD EXCLUSIONS:
      - Alternative explanations or situations that invalidate the label.
      - If triggered → decision must be NO or INSUFFICIENT.
   B) ALTERNATIVE EXPLANATIONS:
      - Strong competing diagnoses or rule-out patterns.
      - These apply penalties and/or cap the maximum possible decision.
      - This should include an EXTENSIVE list of all differential diagnoses and the diagnosis pattern associated with the differential diagnosis.
      - There should be large penalties for specific differential diagnoses patterns.
      - Develop the rubrics that are very strict for differential diagnoses.

6) Evaluation order MUST be:
   (1) Hard exclusions
   (2) Alternative explanations
   (3) Positive evidence scoring
   (4) Decision thresholds

7) The rubric must be realistic for administrative claims
   and avoid reliance on unavailable data.

8) You will name administrative code concept sets that are required for each rule.
   BE CAREFUL TO BE VERY INCLUSIVE! Review each part of the rule criteria to ensure every part that needs a concept set is included.
   a) VERY IMPORTANT: Including too many concept sets is MUCH better than including too few concept sets.
   b) The name should be VERY EXPLICIT so that you can use those named sets in the future to populate concepts into them.
    i) ALWAYS end concept sets for diagnosis codes with the words "DIAGNOSIS_CODES"
    ii) ALWAYS end concept sets for procedure codes with the words "PROCEDURE_CODES"
    iii) ALWAYS end concept sets for drug/medication codes with the words "MEDICATION_CODES"
    iiii) ALWAYS end concept sets for visit codes with the words "VISIT_CODES"
   c) Use _'s between words in the conceptset name for readability. ALWAYS spell out acronyms as this produce better clarity of the idea for the concept set.
   d) If you include a visit type, always include that as a concept set in the list.
    Example: for a criteria like: "≥2 outpatient OR 1 inpatient SLE diagnoses separated by ≥30 days", you need to
    include a concept set for outpatient visits and one for inpatient visits, as well as SLE concepts.
   e) If there are events to be excluded in the criteria, always include concept sets for the exclusions
    Example: for the criteria "Diagnosis codes for hypoglycemia with coma OR complex migraine with aura OR seizure disorder without concurrent stroke treatment evidence" you need to include a concept set for stroke treatment evidence.

9) NEVER refer to another rule or rules within a rule.  Example - never create a rule like:
"Only transient ischemic attack diagnoses (TIA) WITHOUT any evidence rules satisfied after +30 days"

10) In rules such as "≥2 diagnoses of interstitial cystitis WITHOUT any bladder-cancer-specific treatment codes" ALWAYS ensure that there is a
concept_sets_needed entry for the exclusion part, in this example, "WITHOUT any bladder-cancer-specific treatment codes" needs to have an element in
concept_sets_needed, in this example: bladder_cancer_specific_treatment_codes MUST be included in concept_sets_needed as well as any other concept_sets_needed.

11) ALWAYS put specific date constraints for criteria involving the cohort_start_date (index date)
      NEVER use an ambiguous criteria such as "Diagnosis of complex migraine with aura within window" - ALWAYS use a format similar to:
      "Diagnosis of complex migraine with aura within -1 to +5 of the index date"
      NOTE: -1 and +5 are example days - always use appropriate days for the specific criteria.

12) IMPORTANT: EVERY criteria statement MUST have a date range around the index event (cohort_start_date).
  Explicitly put in EVERY criteria statement the words "within N1 to N2 of index" where N1 is the start of the period and N2 is the end of the period.

13) If there are multiple events, ensure that specific date constraints for criteria (labeled in the JSON as "criteria") include and are explicit for each event in the criteria.
    Example: "Any severe sepsis or septic shock diagnosis within -1 to +5 of index
              with antibiotic treatment within +1 to +5 of severe sepsis or septic shock diagnosis
              without concurrent neutropenia-specific treatment within 0 to +7 of severe sepsis or septic shock diagnosis"

